# Project Documentation

## Overview

This project is an end-to-end (E2E) test automation framework for the SauceDemo web application.

It uses WebdriverIO with TypeScript and Cucumber to automate browser interactions and validate the application's main user workflows.

The project follows the Page Object Model (POM) design pattern to keep selectors, actions, and validations organized and maintainable.

## Technology Choices and Trade-offs

The framework was built using **WebdriverIO, TypeScript, Cucumber/Gherkin, Yarn, and Allure Reports**. WebdriverIO was selected for browser automation, while TypeScript provides type safety and better maintainability. Cucumber/Gherkin allows test scenarios to be written in a clear, business-readable format, and Allure provides detailed test execution reports. Yarn was used for dependency management.

The **Page Object Model (POM)** was adopted to separate page elements, actions, and validations, improving reusability and maintainability. The main trade-off is that this structure requires more initial setup and additional files compared with writing tests directly in step definitions.

The tests target the SauceDemo application using Chrome. Credentials and the base URL are managed through environment variables in a `.env` file. The framework currently focuses on UI end-to-end testing rather than API testing or cross-browser coverage.

## Tech Stack

- WebdriverIO
- TypeScript
- Node.js
- Yarn
- Cucumber
- Gherkin
- Expect-WebdriverIO
- Allure Reports
- Google Chrome

## Prerequisites

Make sure the following tools are installed:

- [Node.js](https://nodejs.org/)
- [Yarn](https://yarnpkg.com/)
- [Google Chrome](https://www.google.com/chrome/)

## Installation

### 1.Open the project folder

Extract the project ZIP file, or clone the GitHub repository:
`git clone https://github.com/Yuno94/wdio-e2e-project.git`

Open the wdio-e2e-project folder in Visual Studio Code or PowerShell.
Run all commands from the project root directory.

### 2. Install Yarn

If Yarn is not already installed, open PowerShell and run:

```
npm.cmd install --global yarn@1.22.22
```

Verify the installation:

```
yarn.cmd --version
```

### 3. Install project dependencies

From the project root directory, run:

```
yarn.cmd install
```

This installs all dependencies defined in `package.json`.

### 3. Configure environment variables

Create a .env file in the project root directory, using .env.example as a template.

Example:

BASE_URL=https://www.saucedemo.com
SAUCE_USERNAME=standard_user
SAUCE_PASSWORD=secret_sauce
SAUCE_USERNAME2=locked_out_user

These variables provide the application URL and test login credentials.

Important: The .env file contains environment-specific configuration and should not be committed to GitHub. It is excluded through .gitignore.

## Running Tests Locally

To execute the complete WebdriverIO test suite:

```
yarn.cmd wdio
```

To execute only the purchase scenario:
```
yarn.cmd wdio --cucumberOpts.tags "@purchase"
```
To execute only scenarios tagged with @negative:
```
yarn.cmd wdio --cucumberOpts.tags "@negative"
```
Tests are written using Cucumber feature files with Gherkin syntax and are located in:

```text
test/features/
```

Tests will run in the browser configured in `wdio.conf.ts`.

## Cucumber and Gherkin

This project uses Cucumber for Behavior-Driven Development (BDD).

Feature files describe application behavior using Given, When, and Then steps.

Step definitions connect the Gherkin steps to the automation code and are stored in:

```text
test/step-definitions/
```

## Page Object Model (POM)

The project follows the Page Object Model design pattern to separate page selectors, actions, and validations.

The test/pages/ directory contains page-specific files organized by application functionality.

-.pom.ts – Page selectors and element references.

-.actions.ts – Reusable browser interactions.

-.validations.ts – Assertions and page validations.

The base-page.ts file contains shared browser page functionality.

Step definitions call the corresponding page methods instead of directly interacting with browser elements.

## Allure Reports

This project uses Allure to generate detailed and interactive test execution reports.

### 1. Install Allure dependencies

```
yarn.cmd add --dev @wdio/allure-reporter allure-commandline
```

### 2. Configure the Allure reporter

In `wdio.conf.ts`, configure the reporters section:

```typescript
reporters: [
    'spec',
    ['allure', {
        outputDir: 'allure-results',
        disableWebdriverStepsReporting: false,
        disableWebdriverScreenshotsReporting: false,
    }],
],
```

### 3. Execute the tests

```
yarn.cmd wdio
```

Raw test results will be generated in the `allure-results` directory.

### 4. Generate the Allure HTML report

```
yarn.cmd allure generate allure-results --clean
```

### 5. Open the Allure report

```
yarn.cmd allure open allure-report
```

### Allure Report Features

The report provides information about:

- Passed, failed, and skipped tests
- Test execution details
- Failure messages and stack traces
- Test execution history
- Screenshots and browser steps, when configured

### Screenshots and Video Reporting

The framework includes screenshot and video reporting configuration through WebdriverIO reporters.

Screenshots can be attached to Allure reports when a test step fails.

Video recording is handled by the configured video reporter. FFmpeg must be installed and available on the system for video files to be generated.

The availability of video recordings depends on the reporter configuration and execution environment.

### GitHub Actions – Continuous Integration

The project includes a GitHub Actions workflow:

```
.github/workflows/e2e.yml
```

The workflow is configured to execute the E2E tests automatically when changes are pushed to the main branch or when a pull request targets main.

It performs the following steps:

   - Checks out the repository.

   - Sets up Node.js.

   - Installs Yarn.

   - Installs project dependencies.

   - Executes the WebdriverIO test suite.

   - Uploads Allure results as a workflow artifact.

GitHub Secrets

The .env file is not committed to GitHub. Instead, GitHub Actions receives the required environment variables through repository secrets.

Configure the following secrets under:

### GitHub Secrets 
The .env file is not committed to GitHub. Instead, GitHub Actions receives the required environment variables through repository secrets.

Configure the following secrets under:

GitHub Repository → Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions
Secret name      Purpose
BASE_URL         SauceDemo application URL
SAUCE_USERNAME   Standard test user
SAUCE_PASSWORD   Test user password
SAUCE_USERNAME2  Locked-out test user



## Code Quality – ESLint

The project uses ESLint to identify potential code issues and maintain consistent coding standards.

To check the project:

```
yarn.cmd lint
```

To automatically fix supported linting issues:

```
yarn.cmd lint:fix
```

